<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cipriano Castro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1924">1924</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cipriano Castro was a Venezuelan military leader from the Andean state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tachira">Táchira</a> who seized power in 1899 and ruled as president until 1908. His refusal to pay foreign debts led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> Venezuelan ports in 1902–1903; while he was abroad for medical treatment in 1908, his vice-president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-vicente-gomez">Juan Vicente Gómez</a> took power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cipriano-castro.jpg" alt="Cipriano Castro" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicola Perscheid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cipriano_Castro_1908.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Cipriano Castro Ruiz was born on 12 October 1858 in Capacho Viejo, in the Andean state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tachira">Táchira</a>, the only son of a mid-level farmer. He studied at a seminary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, Colombia, then worked for a trading company and as a cowboy.</p><p>In 1886 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tachira">Táchira</a> as a soldier in a regional autonomy rising, defeated government forces at Capacho Viejo and Rubio, and was made a general; at a funeral he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-vicente-gomez">Juan Vicente Gómez</a>, his later ally. After he failed to carry a campaign against the government to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> in 1892, he spent seven years in Colombia, amassing a fortune in illegal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> trading and recruiting a private army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In October 1899 Castro marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> in the Restorative Liberal Revolution and seized power, naming Gómez his vice-president. His rule was marked by frequent rebellions, the murder or exile of opponents and disputes with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and European powers. In 1901 the banker Manuel Antonio Matos led the Liberating Revolution, backed by foreign companies, which brought the government close to collapse; Castro's troops broke the Siege of La Victoria in November 1902.</p><p>Within weeks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> blockaded Venezuelan ports over unpaid debts and damages to their citizens. Castro assumed the Monroe Doctrine would restrain them, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a>'s government let the action go ahead. After two Venezuelan ships were sunk and the coast bombarded, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> pressed for a settlement, and an agreement signed in Washington on 13 February 1903 lifted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> and committed 30 per cent of customs duties to claims.</p><p>On 22 February 1904 the Permanent Court of Arbitration at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> held that the blockading powers were entitled to preferential treatment, a decision that helped produce the Roosevelt Corollary. In 1908 a dispute with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> led to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Seriously ill with a kidney problem, Castro left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> for treatment in December 1908. On 19 December Gómez seized power himself and ended the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Castro spent years in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, trying repeatedly to raise a force to return, and settled in Santurce, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, in 1918. He died there on 4 December 1924. He was the first Venezuelan from the Andes to rule the country and the first of four military strongmen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tachira">Táchira</a> to do so over the next 46 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cipriano Castro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Civil wars and revolts continued until <b>Cipriano Castro</b> and Juan Vicente Gómez, leaders from the Andean state of Táchira, seized Caracas in 1899.</blockquote><a href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tachira">Táchira</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-puerto-rico">San Juan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=7.83,-72.08&amp;year=1900" aria-label="Táchira on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2557 1871 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cipriano Castro"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3007 2141h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3007 2141h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=7.83,-72.08&amp;year=1900">What was here in 1900? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
